**2.12 Theorem.** The injective spaces are exactly the continuous lattices.

This theorem is an immediate consequence of the preceding results: an injective space is a retract of a power of $\mathbb{O}$. But $\mathbb{O}$ is a finite lattice $(\perp \sqsubseteq \top)$, and so the given space is a continuous lattice under its induced topology. On the other hand a continuous lattice is injective. It remains then to prove 2.9–2.11.

Fixed points of an idempotent $e$ on a complete lattice form the subtype **`IdempotentFixedPoint e`**. In [`IdempotentFixedPoint.lean`](IdempotentFixedPoint.lean), namespace **`IdempotentFixedPoint`** supplies **`supremumSet`** (the join on fixed points: apply $e$ to the ambient supremum), **`isLUB_sSup`**, **`completeLattice`**, **`coe_sSup`**, directed-sup preservation for inclusion and retraction, **`retraction`**, **`isContinuousLattice`**, and **`scottTopology`** (via Proposition 2.10). The proof **`theorem_2_12`** assembles these with Constructions 2.9–2.11 and **`ContinuousLattice.orderIso`** (Definition 2.3 note).

